NEARS-1475 — QA live probe, Widgetbook release web build (Chrome/Playwright, light mode)
Story: components/nmoduleswitcher/nmoduleswitcher/playground?knobs={error:true}

Error card measured from the live screenshot: x 306..584, y 57..188.

--- control experiment (same session, live DOM reads between steps) ---
step: tap "Switch module"      -> grep "could not" => node present  (overlay OPEN)
step: click (445, 84)          -> inside the card, on the MESSAGE row
step: grep "could not"         -> (no match)
step: grep "switch"            -> trigger node back => overlay CLOSED

step: click (445, 156)         -> inside the card, on the RETRY button
step: grep "could not"         -> (no match)
step: grep "switch"            -> trigger node back => overlay CLOSED

EXPECTED: a tap on the inert error message text should be absorbed by the (disabled)
menu item; only retry / tap-outside / back should dismiss.
OBSERVED: a tap anywhere inside the error card dismisses the overlay, so a user aiming
at the message loses the card and has to reopen it to reach "Try again".

Side effect on evidence: because both taps close the overlay, "overlay closed" alone
cannot prove onRetry fired. Retry being a live control was proven separately by the
hover repaint (see bug-retry-not-exposed-to-a11y.log); onRetry actually firing is
covered by the unit test 'the retry button fires onRetry and dismisses the overlay'.


================================================================================
DELTA RE-QA CYCLE 1 (after the _NErrorEntry host swap) — STILL REPRODUCES.
Rebuilt from the same worktree: flutter test 855/855 (run BEFORE the web build),
flutter analyze clean, fresh `flutter build web --release` served on a NEW port
(8124) so no stale asset could be reused from the first run's origin.
================================================================================

The engineer's non-repro hypothesis was: "the card is only ~280px wide, so x=445
may have landed on the modal barrier". That is now DISPROVED by direct measurement
of the live semantics tree — and my original card bounds are CONFIRMED, not corrected.

--- card bounds, measured independently this run ---
retry button node   : x 321.2 .. 569.2  (w 248, h 44)   role=button
message text node   : x 353.2 .. 569.2  (y 77 .. 95, h 18)
card = content + the component's own EdgeInsets.symmetric(horizontal: space4=16):
      left  = 321.2 - 16 = 305.2
      right = 569.2 + 16 = 585.2      => card is 280 wide, spanning x 305.2 .. 585.2
First-run pixel scan said the white card ran x 306 .. 584. The two agree.
x = 445 is 140 px inside the left edge and 140 px clear of the right edge.
It was never on the barrier. 305 + 280 = 585, not 445.

--- the decisive re-test: click resolved BY VISIBLE TEXT, not by coordinate ---
This run did not compute a click point at all. It located the message's OWN
semantics element by exact textContent and clicked the centre of its live rect:

  taptext "We could not load your modules."
      target rect l=353.2 t=77.0 w=216.0 h=18.0 -> click centre (461.2, 86.0)
  grep  "try again"   -> (no match)
  textbox "We could not load your modules." -> []
  grep  "switch"      -> trigger node back   => overlay CLOSED

A point that is the geometric centre of the message text's own rect cannot be the
modal barrier. The overlay still dismisses.

--- how much of the card is transparent to taps (new characterization) ---
  click (461.2, 86.0)  message text centre                      -> DISMISSED
  click (461, 102)     the 12 px gap between message and button -> DISMISSED
  click (312, 170)     card's bottom-left padding column        -> DISMISSED
  hover (461, 86)      message row, 260x30 rect                 -> 0 px change
                       (pixel-identical census before/after: idle and hover both
                        #FFFFFF 6758 / #BA1A1A 29 / #6A6A78 28) => genuinely inert
  click far outside (900, 400)                                  -> DISMISSED (correct)

So EVERY non-button point inside the card passes the pointer through to the barrier;
only the NButton absorbs. The new host made the panel structurally tap-free, which
removes an opaque hit-test surface rather than adding one — the fall-through is if
anything more certain than before, not less.
